---
import Docs from "../../../layouts/Docs.astro";

const locations = [
  { name: "docs/drafts/<slug>/", set: "Agent / PO, while shaping", means: "Being shaped — an idea, not a commitment. May be incomplete; nothing may depend on it yet, so it may simply be deleted.", to: "→ docs/specs/ (the decider's go), or deleted / archived as killed (\"abandoned\")." },
  { name: "docs/specs/<slug>/", set: "The decider, by moving the folder", means: "Approved for build — the move itself, recorded in git, is the record. No status field tracks progress here; the folder's presence is the state, whether the build hasn't started yet or is well underway.", to: "→ docs/knowledge/ + docs/archive/ (ships), or → docs/archive/ (killed)." },
  { name: "docs/knowledge/<slug>/ + docs/archive/<slug>/", set: "Graduation, wired to the implementation PR", means: "Shipped. archive/ holds the contract, verbatim, with an acceptance-results link, status: shipped, read-only forever — what was promised. knowledge/ is the living, present-tense description — what it became.", to: "Terminal." },
  { name: "docs/archive/<slug>/ (alone)", set: "A deliberate kill, or an abandoned draft", means: "Dropped. Archived with a one-line tombstone stating why (\"abandoned\" for a draft dropped before ship) and status: killed. The slug stays burned.", to: "Terminal." },
];
---
<Docs title="Reference — lifecycle & states">
  <div class="doc-kicker">Reference · Lifecycle</div>
  <h1>State is location</h1>
  <p class="doc-lead">
    <strong>Where a spec lives is its state</strong> — there is no frontmatter <code>status</code>
    field to keep in step with the folder. Four folders under <code>docs/</code> carry the
    whole lifecycle, and a spec is in exactly one of them. Four <strong>moves</strong> carry it
    from an idea to a shipped, archived contract; <em>reshape</em> is a normal move, not a
    failure. The sequence isn't prescribed — shape, approve, build, and graduate can be one
    commit or several branches; Specline reads folders, not order. The only place a
    <code>status</code> field still appears is <code>archive/</code> (<code>shipped | killed</code>)
    — location can't tell those two apart there. A <code>status:</code> line written against an
    earlier canon (including <code>ratified</code>, <code>building</code>, <code>blocked</code>)
    is recognised silently.
  </p>

  <h2>The locations</h2>
  <p>Each location names what's true, who moves a spec into it, and where it can go next:</p>
  <div class="tbl-wrap">
    <table class="book-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Location</th><th>What it means</th><th>Who moves it</th><th>Transitions</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {locations.map((s) => (
          <tr>
            <td><code>{s.name}</code></td>
            <td>{s.means}</td>
            <td>{s.set}</td>
            <td>{s.to}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The four moves</h2>
  <p>The lifecycle loop is the feature's journey <em>across</em> the gates — distinct from the experimental, opt-in build loop that can run <em>inside</em> the build step (unattended builds only; see <a href="/docs/reference/altitudes">acceptance altitudes</a>):</p>

  <div class="bcard">
    <h3>Shape <span class="gate-tag gate-human">agent + PO</span></h3>
    <p>An agent drafts the contract in <code>docs/drafts/</code>; the PO edits. Only parse-level integrity runs here — incomplete is what a draft is for.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="bcard">
    <h3>Approve <span class="gate-tag gate-human">decider · the first gate</span></h3>
    <p>A named human reads the whole contract in one sitting (<em>One sitting</em>) and gives it their <strong>go</strong> — moving the folder from <code>docs/drafts/</code> to <code>docs/specs/</code>, in a commit made or approved by the decider, on whichever branch the work is on. Git records who and when. The spec does <em>not</em> have to merge first.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="bcard">
    <h3>Build <span class="gate-tag gate-human">builder · attended by default</span></h3>
    <p>The builder works from <code>docs/specs/&lt;slug&gt;/</code> toward the Goal, against the acceptance checks. <strong>Attended</strong> (the default) the decider is reachable: the builder disagrees out loud and the spec is amended by agreement. <strong>Unattended</strong> (<code>build: unattended</code>, experimental) it runs the autonomous loop and escalates back to a human at the first of two triggers — <code>loop_budget</code> exhausted or <code>stale_after</code> reached — and a contradiction parks it <code>blocked</code> in <code>status.md</code>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="bcard">
    <h3>Graduate <span class="gate-tag gate-human">agent · wired to the impl PR</span></h3>
    <p>An agent-executed editing pass, triggered structurally by the implementation PR: archives the final <code>spec.md</code> as <code>shipped</code>, writes the present-tense <code>knowledge/</code> doc, and deletes the spec folder. The human acceptance gate reviews the graduated doc — "is this what I decided?"</p>
  </div>

  <h2>One branch, or two</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>normal shape is one branch</strong>: the spec folder is created in <code>drafts/</code>,
    the decider's go moves it to <code>specs/</code>, the build lands against it, graduation runs,
    and one pull request carries all of it. Its approving merge is the record of contract, build,
    and graduation.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>spec landed ahead of the build</strong> is the two-branch shape: the move to
    <code>specs/</code> lands first, and the implementation PR follows, referencing the slug.
    Reach for it when shaping runs well ahead of building, when several branches are shaping in
    parallel and a slug needs claiming early, or when an unattended runner requires a landed
    baseline. It is a legal shape, not the rule — and the slug freezes when the spec lands on the
    merge target, not at the decider's go.
  </p>

  <div class="callout"><span class="ci">▸</span><p><strong>Reshape is a normal move.</strong> The spec on the branch being built is the contract, and it changes as the build teaches. <strong>Attended:</strong> the builder says so in product terms, the decider agrees, the builder edits <code>spec.md</code> in the branch — acceptance checks included, proposed before they're changed — and the diff is the record. No folder move, no separate PR. <strong>Unattended (experimental):</strong> there's no conversation to have, so it's a handback — set <code>status.md</code>'s State to <code>blocked</code>, land a decider-approved spec-amendment commit touching only the spec folder (resetting <code>stale_after</code> in the same commit), then resume.</p></div>

  <div class="callout"><span class="ci">▸</span><p>Up: <a href="/handbook/building">handbook — building</a>. The law: <a href="/spec.md">the canon, "Lifecycle"</a>.</p></div>
</Docs>
